; cat README.md | head -30"]], "final_message": "The likely cause is that the `ts` column mixes three timestamp formats. The file only has three rows, and each one uses a different format:\n\n| id | ts | meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `2026-03-01T10:00:00Z` | 10:00 UTC |\n| 2 | `2026-03-01T10:05:00` | no zone given, so it's ambiguous |\n| 3 | `2026-03-01T04:10:00-06:00` | 10:10 UTC, written in a UTC-6 local time |\n\nRows 1 and 3 are 10 minutes apart in real time. Read naively, row 3 looks like 04:10, which is six hours earlier. That is the kind of jump you're seeing.\n\nRow 2 has no zone, so each tool guesses. Pandas leaves it naive, and mixing it with aware values either errors or falls back to `object` dtype. JavaScript's `Date` and many charting libraries treat it as the viewer's local time. Its position on the chart then depends on the machine rendering it. That would explain activity that moves by several hours between charts.\n\nA chart that groups by hour, or that drops the offset by formatting the string, will also put row 3 at 04:00 instead of 10:00.\n\n**Fix:**\n1. Find out what zone the naive rows were recorded in. I can't tell that from the file. The exporter or its docs should say. If it's UTC, treat them as UTC.\n2. Normalize everything to UTC on load. In pandas that's `pd.to_datetime(df.ts, utc=True)`, which treats naive values as UTC. If they're really local time, localize them first.\n3. Better still, fix the exporter to always write ISO 8601 with an explicit offset or `Z`.\n\nI only have these three rows. If the real export is larger, the share of naive rows and the spread of offsets will show how bad the shifts are. If you'd like, I can write the normalization step or check the full file.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}